1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,170
Monday morning at Kestrel Charging.

2
00:00:05,795 --> 00:00:10,142
The problem: every new API waits for
one person to review it by hand,

3
00:00:10,192 --> 00:00:14,603
and the standards live on a wiki page
nobody reads before they deploy.

4
00:00:14,653 --> 00:00:17,854
This part shows Rob turning
the standard into code,

5
00:00:17,904 --> 00:00:21,614
so the cluster checks every API
against it on every change.

6
00:00:22,776 --> 00:00:27,501
This is Kestrel Charging, a fictional
network of electric vehicle chargers.

7
00:00:27,551 --> 00:00:32,531
Right now every new API waits for Rob,
the platform lead, to review it by hand:

8
00:00:32,581 --> 00:00:35,015
about two hours each, six in the queue.

9
00:00:35,065 --> 00:00:39,536
The standards live on a wiki page
that nobody reads before they deploy.

10
00:00:39,586 --> 00:00:43,420
In this series we replace that
queue with Tyk API Governance,

11
00:00:43,470 --> 00:00:46,986
running entirely through the
Tyk Operator on Kubernetes.

12
00:00:48,136 --> 00:00:49,082
Why it matters.

13
00:00:49,132 --> 00:00:52,405
In March, an API went live
without authentication.

14
00:00:52,455 --> 00:00:56,259
A bot scraped it and took a
system down for forty minutes.

15
00:00:56,309 --> 00:01:01,242
Rob wrote that lesson down afterwards,
but a document cannot stop a deploy.

16
00:01:02,401 --> 00:01:04,088
Two people carry the story.

17
00:01:04,138 --> 00:01:07,241
Ines is a backend engineer
on the payments squad.

18
00:01:07,291 --> 00:01:10,522
She has to ship the Kestrel
Payments API by Friday,

19
00:01:10,572 --> 00:01:14,381
because a partner with nine
hundred vans launches on Monday.

20
00:01:14,431 --> 00:01:16,890
Rob owns the cluster and the standards.

21
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:21,908
Watch how the work passes between them:
Rob sets the rules, Ines follows them,

22
00:01:21,958 --> 00:01:26,605
and when a rule turns out to be wrong,
Rob changes the rule, not her API.

23
00:01:27,765 --> 00:01:30,439
Kestrel's APIs are Kubernetes resources.

24
00:01:30,489 --> 00:01:32,959
Each one is a Tyk OAS API definition.

25
00:01:33,009 --> 00:01:37,453
The Tyk Operator watches them and
keeps the Tyk Dashboard in sync,

26
00:01:37,503 --> 00:01:40,314
and the Dashboard configures the gateways.

27
00:01:41,467 --> 00:01:46,476
The last column, preflight status, is
the governance verdict for each API.

28
00:01:46,526 --> 00:01:50,919
Right now every API says skipped,
because no standard exists yet.

29
00:01:50,969 --> 00:01:56,046
Nothing is checking these APIs, including
the one Ines pushed this morning.

30
00:01:57,252 --> 00:01:58,939
Here is her API definition.

31
00:01:58,989 --> 00:02:02,349
The Tyk section at the bottom
configures the gateway:

32
00:02:02,399 --> 00:02:04,794
a listen path and an upstream address.

33
00:02:04,844 --> 00:02:07,689
There is no authentication
and no rate limit.

34
00:02:07,739 --> 00:02:11,743
That is exactly the mistake from
March, and nobody has noticed.

35
00:02:16,185 --> 00:02:17,763
The Tyk Dashboard agrees.

36
00:02:17,813 --> 00:02:22,777
Its Governance Overview shows three APIs:
none compliant, none non-compliant.

37
00:02:22,827 --> 00:02:27,986
Everything is not applicable, because
there is nothing to be compliant with yet.

38
00:02:29,378 --> 00:02:30,918
Half past nine.

39
00:02:30,968 --> 00:02:31,768
Rob.

40
00:02:34,984 --> 00:02:36,674
Rob's hands on the keyboard.

41
00:02:36,724 --> 00:02:41,148
He writes the standard as a Tyk ruleset,
which is a Kubernetes resource.

42
00:02:41,198 --> 00:02:44,690
A ruleset is a named list of
rules that APIs must follow.

43
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:49,786
Because it is a manifest, it lives in Git
and gets reviewed like any other change.

44
00:02:50,955 --> 00:02:53,656
He doesn't write the rules from scratch.

45
00:02:53,706 --> 00:02:57,233
This ruleset extends the
OWASP API Security ruleset,

46
00:02:57,283 --> 00:03:01,841
a widely used set of API security
checks, and includes all of them.

47
00:03:02,990 --> 00:03:07,721
It is bound to a category, payments,
rather than to Ines's API by name.

48
00:03:07,771 --> 00:03:10,213
Every API tagged payments is checked,

49
00:03:10,263 --> 00:03:13,648
including the two her squad
will ship next quarter.

50
00:03:14,804 --> 00:03:18,781
The action says what happens when
an API breaks an error-level rule.

51
00:03:18,831 --> 00:03:20,735
Warn means the developer is told,

52
00:03:20,785 --> 00:03:23,934
in the Dashboard and in the
terminal when they deploy.

53
00:03:23,984 --> 00:03:25,059
Nothing is blocked.

54
00:03:25,109 --> 00:03:27,546
We'll see the stricter option on Thursday.

55
00:03:28,698 --> 00:03:33,074
Rob applies it, exactly the way he
deploys anything else on the cluster.

56
00:03:34,225 --> 00:03:38,798
The Tyk Operator created the ruleset in
the Dashboard and wrote the Dashboard's

57
00:03:38,848 --> 00:03:40,788
identifier back onto the resource.

58
00:03:40,838 --> 00:03:42,543
The sync status is Successful.

59
00:03:46,997 --> 00:03:48,967
In the Dashboard, under Governance,

60
00:03:49,017 --> 00:03:53,470
the same ruleset appears with its payments
category and the deployment warning

61
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:54,320
switched on.

62
00:03:54,212 --> 00:03:56,586
Rob never opened this screen to create it.

63
00:04:02,209 --> 00:04:06,800
Opening it lists every rule the template
brought in, each with a severity.

64
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:10,123
Error-level rules decide
whether an API is compliant.

65
00:04:10,173 --> 00:04:11,503
Warnings are advisory.

66
00:04:12,875 --> 00:04:15,424
Now the part generic linters can't do.

67
00:04:15,474 --> 00:04:19,322
March's outage was gateway
configuration, not API design.

68
00:04:19,372 --> 00:04:24,657
Rob writes custom rules in the same format
that look inside x-tyk-api-gateway,

69
00:04:24,707 --> 00:04:26,503
Tyk's own gateway settings.

70
00:04:26,553 --> 00:04:30,196
This one checks the upstream
section for a rate limit.

71
00:04:31,347 --> 00:04:36,409
At error severity, an API without an
upstream rate limit cannot be compliant.

72
00:04:37,561 --> 00:04:40,058
And every rule carries how-to-fix text.

73
00:04:40,108 --> 00:04:43,846
That text is what developers
will see next to the failure,

74
00:04:43,896 --> 00:04:47,569
so the standard arrives with
instructions, not just a no.

75
00:04:48,739 --> 00:04:52,211
The second ruleset loads
those rules from a ConfigMap,

76
00:04:52,261 --> 00:04:56,320
so one file serves the cluster,
the Dashboard and the pipeline.

77
00:04:56,370 --> 00:05:01,473
It covers both payments and internal APIs,
and its action is none: report only.

78
00:05:01,523 --> 00:05:06,234
Rob wants to see how the existing APIs
measure up before he warns anyone.

79
00:05:07,417 --> 00:05:11,902
He applies it, and within seconds
every tagged API has been evaluated.

80
00:05:11,952 --> 00:05:13,716
All three are non-compliant.

81
00:05:13,766 --> 00:05:18,640
Ines's payments API scores twenty-five
out of a hundred against Rob's rules,

82
00:05:18,690 --> 00:05:21,426
and forty-three against
the OWASP baseline.

83
00:05:22,565 --> 00:05:24,967
And Rob learns something he didn't know.

84
00:05:25,017 --> 00:05:28,705
The tariffs API from the March
incident is authenticated now,

85
00:05:28,755 --> 00:05:31,586
but it still has no rate
limit on its upstream.

86
00:05:36,436 --> 00:05:40,686
Two rulesets, two commits, about
fifteen minutes of Rob's time.

87
00:05:40,736 --> 00:05:43,280
One warns developers when they deploy.

88
00:05:43,330 --> 00:05:44,850
The other only reports.

89
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:47,240
Rob has not opened Ines's API once,

90
00:05:47,290 --> 00:05:50,653
and tomorrow morning she
will find out on her own.
